Wang,
  Discussion of a model with orthogonal exogenous variables played a central role in the debate between Gerbing and Anderson and Fornell and Yi during the early 1990's.  It is not clear from your question what problem you are trying to solve when you say "deal with".  If you want to recoup the df, you need to model the exogenous variables.  By default, lavaan will not do this.  You need to use the fixed.x=FALSE parameter which is documented in the Lavaan Options help file.  I believe that you then have to specify all covariances except the one that is zero.  I am not aware of any shortcut for that.  If desired, instead of implicitly fixing a parameter to zero by omitting it from the model specification, you can instead explicitly fix it to zero using syntax like x ~~ 0 * y where 'x' and 'y' are variable names.  Another common scripting trick is to include a line like 'x ~~ y' and then comment it out so that it is there for model comparison if you need it and also as a reminder for human readers that the parameter has been omitted.

Fornell, C. & Yi, Y. (1992).  Assumptions of the two-step approach to latent variable modeling.  Sociological Methods & Research, 20, 291-320.  doi: 10.1177/0049124192020003003

Keith
