     Many circumstances could lead to this residual covariance between predictor and outcome variables. Just including the free residual covariance may only be hiding the real problem. For instance unreliability ("measurement error") in teh predictor may lead to this, in which case it would be better to address the unreliability problem than to "paper over" the problem with a residual covariance. Or it may be that an omitted predictor, correlated with the predictor in question, may be responsible. Or a selection problem might have crept into the research design. As Carl said, the causal inference literature would say that this is classic endogeneity, and their solution would be to (1) include a latent variable which jointly causes both the predictor and the outcome variable, and then (2) as Yen says, find an instrumental variable (or some more sophisticated technique) that will allow you to estimate this augmented model. One caution, however: the causal inference literature deals primary in nonparameteric estimation, while your typical SEM is linear in the parameters, so there is not a strict one-to-one betweed the two literatures (causal inference and structural equation modeling).

